i-paya-d01.jpg"}},{"id":"yangon_ministers_building","areaId":"yangon","name":"Ministers' Building","category":"landmark","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":16.77556,"lng":96.16583},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"yangon_ministers_building","tickets":[{"vendor":"Official","url":"https://thesecretariatyangon.com/","official":true}],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":29,"wikiPageviews":302,"wikidataSitelinks":4,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["ministersbuilding","yangon"],"bestLight":"golden","summary":"The Ministers' Building; officially the Ministers' Office, also known as The Secretariat or Secretariat Yangon, was the administrative seat of British Burma, in downtown Yangon, Burma and is the spot where Aung San and eight cabinet ministers were assassinated.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Ministers' Building","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Secretariat_Office.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Secretariat_Office.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"Aungphone","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secretariat_Office.jpg"}},{"id":"yangon_musmeah_yeshua_synagogue","areaId":"yangon","name":"Musmeah Yeshua Synagogue","category":"monument","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":16.77427,"lng":96.15398},"visitMinutes":45,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"yangon_musmeah_yeshua_synagogue","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":1,"commonsNearby":14,"wikiPageviews":173,"wikidataSitelinks":10,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["musmeahyeshuasynagogue","yangon"],"hours":{"week":[{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"The Musmeah Yeshua Synagogue is a synagogue, located in downtown Yangon, Myanmar. Completed in 1896 to replace an earlier wooden synagogue that was erected in 1854, the current stone synagogue is the only synagogue in Myanmar.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Musmeah Yeshua Synagogue","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Musmeah_Yeshua_synagogue_altar.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Musmeah_Yeshua_synagogue_altar.jpg","w":1200,"h":798,"author":"Esme Vos","license":"CC BY 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musmeah_Yeshua_synagogue_altar.jpg"}},{"id":"yangon_bahadur_shah_zafar_grave_dispute","areaId":"yangon","name":"Bahadur Shah Zafar grave dispute","category":"monument","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":16.78981,"lng":96.14989},"visitMinutes":30,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"yangon_bahadur_shah_zafar_grave_dispute","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":0,"commonsNearby":9,"wikiPageviews":770,"wikidataSitelinks":2,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["bahadurshahzafargravedispute","yangon"],"bestLight":"morning","summary":"The location of the grave of Bahadur Shah Zafar, the last Mughal emperor at Yangon, Myanmar, is in dispute. He was buried in the back of his enclosure, but by 1903, no one knew where. After some protests, the British were forced to construct a stone slab to mark the site of his grave.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Bahadur Shah Zafar grave dispute","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Mazar_of_Bahadur_Shah_Zafar.jpg/1280px-Mazar_of_Bahadur_Shah_Zafar.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Mazar_of_Bahadur_Shah_Zafar.jpg/500px-Mazar_of_Bahadur_Shah_Zafar.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"EmeraldRange","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"http